.TH FOLD 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
fold \- wrap input lines to a given width
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fold
.RB [ \-bs ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR width ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fold
copies each
.I file
to standard output, starting a new line wherever a line would otherwise
be wider than
.I width
columns.
The default width is 80.
.PP
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
.B fold
reads standard input.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, and the
remaining files are still read.
.PP
A line ends at an LF.
Each file starts at column 0.
Apart from the LF bytes it adds, the output is the input byte for byte,
including NUL and CR bytes.
A last line with no LF is written without one, so it runs into the first
line of the next file.
.SS Columns
Without
.BR \-b ,
each byte moves the column, counted from 0, as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
A tab moves to the next multiple of 8.
.IP \(bu 2
A backspace moves back one column, but not before column 0.
.IP \(bu 2
A CR returns to column 0.
.IP \(bu 2
Any other byte, including other control characters and NUL, moves
forward one column.
.PP
In UTF-8 mode, described under
.BR ENVIRONMENT ,
a byte from 0x80 to 0xBF, which can only continue a UTF-8 character,
does not move the column.
Each character is then one column wide, so a break never splits a
character.
Wide and combining characters are not treated specially, and the input
is not checked for being valid UTF-8.
.PP
With
.BR \-b ,
every byte is one column, including tab, backspace, and CR, and a break
can split a UTF-8 character.
.PP
A byte that would end in a column past
.I width
starts a new line instead.
The first byte of an output line is never moved, so a tab at the start of
a line stays there even when
.I width
is less than 8.
.SS Breaking at spaces
Without
.BR \-s ,
a line is broken exactly at
.IR width ,
which can split a word.
.PP
With
.BR \-s ,
when a byte would go past
.IR width ,
.B fold
looks for the last blank, a space or tab, among the bytes already on the
line.
If there is one, the line ends just after it, and the bytes that followed
it, plus the new byte, begin the next line.
Their columns are counted again from column 0.
If there is no blank, the line is broken at
.IR width ,
as without
.BR \-s .
Only a space or a tab is a blank: other white space, such as the
no-break space, is not.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-sw72 ,
and may follow the
.I file
operands.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-w72 ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Long options and the obsolete
.BI \- width
form are not accepted.
.TP
.B \-b
Count bytes rather than columns, as described under
.BR Columns .
.TP
.B \-s
Break at blanks, as described under
.BR "Breaking at spaces" .
.TP
.BI "\-w " width
Wrap at
.I width
columns instead of 80.
The
.I width
is a decimal number from 1 to 10000, with no sign, space, or prefix.
Leading zeros are allowed, and the number is still decimal.
A
.I width
that is not a number, or does not fit in 32 bits, is reported as
.BR "invalid number" ,
and any other value out of range as
.BR "not in 1..10000 range" ,
with exit status 1.
If
.B \-w
is given more than once, the last one applies.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
An unknown option, or a missing option argument, prints the usage
message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-w
are file names.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set decides whether UTF-8 mode is on.
When its value is
.BR C ,
UTF-8 mode is off, and every byte except tab, backspace, and CR is one
column.
Otherwise UTF-8 mode is on when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, which it
is on Windows 10 version 1903 and later, and off when it is not.
The value
.B C
also turns off the conversion of console output described in
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when
.B fold
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or
.IR width ,
a
.I file
could not be opened (a directory is reported as
.BR "Is a directory" )
or read, memory ran out, or standard output could not be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed while
.B fold
was writing to it.
Output is buffered, and when the closed pipe is found only by the final
flush at exit, as with short output, the status is 1 instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
Wrap a text file at word boundaries to 72 columns:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ fold \-s \-w 72 notes.txt >notes.wrapped
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
words are split at the width:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog | fold \-w 18
The quick brown fo
x jumps over the l
azy dog
.EE
.RE
.PP
Break a long line of hex digits into lines of 64 digits:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ fold \-b \-w 64 key.hex >key.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
List a directory as a block of text wrapped at word boundaries:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls | tr \(aq\en\(aq \(aq \(aq | fold \-s \-w 78
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
With
.BR \-s ,
the byte that would go past the width is never itself the blank searched
for.
A blank just past the width therefore starts the next line instead of
ending the current one.
For example,
.B "abcd efgh"
with
.B "\-s \-w 4"
becomes
.BR abcd ,
a line holding only a space, and
.BR efgh .
.PP
Files and standard streams are binary, with no CRLF translation, and
breaks are written as a bare LF.
A CR returns to column 0, so the CR of a CRLF line ending does not count
toward the width, and a line of exactly
.I width
columns followed by CRLF is not broken.
Wrapping a file with CRLF line endings leaves the original CRLF endings
and adds LF-only ones.
Filter the output through
.BR unix2dos (1)
to make them all CRLF.
.PP
When standard output is a console, a NUL byte hides the rest of its
output line, usually including the LF.
.PP
In UTF-8 mode the column count is in characters, not display cells.
A console shows an East Asian wide character in two cells, and
.B fold
counts it as one.
.PP
.B fold
holds the current output line in memory.
A line in which CR or backspace bytes keep the column within the width,
such as a very long overstruck line, is held whole.
.PP
File names may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903
and later, may use backslashes, and may start with a drive letter.
The device names that busybox accepts, such as
.IR /dev/null ,
work as operands; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dos2unix (1),
.BR expand (1),
.BR unix2dos (1)
